COMPLIANT — the artifact obeys the meta-rules of rule authoring and states the parent contract
correctly. Same frontmatter, same three-heading skeleton, same single numbered clause, same
canonical Wiring field set, same Origin shape and within 1.03x the byte size of its counterpart, so
nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests on content.

The modal is MUST / MUST NOT throughout. The DO/DO-NOT block shows two constructs whose outcomes
genuinely differ — a wrapped value against a level-gated raw one — rather than restating the
heading. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech and so is matchable against a transcript. The Why
names the consequence of breach: a wider reader set than the config the value came from, for the
whole retention window. Substantively it holds the parent contract without conditions, naming log
level and deployment environment as non-modifiers and refusing the downstream-scrubber delegation,
with a reason given for each.

TRAPS. (1) This is the denser and slightly longer pole and it argues against a convenience most
engineers want, so a judge holding that shorter or more accommodating rules are better-authored
flags it; length and posture are not meta-compliance. (2) The clause carries two obligations under
one number — emit the digest, and discharge it at the formatting point — which can read as
under-split; they are the two halves of one control, since a digest emitted by a downstream scrubber
is not the same control. (3) The clause text names the very rationalizations it forbids, which can
read as the artifact conceding an exception; each is stated in order to be refused.
